---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Tankless Water Heaters in 2026: Tested";
const description =
  "We measured real flow rates, cold-weather performance and venting costs across 11 tankless water heaters. Gas and electric, condensing and not, ranked.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "rinnai-rx199in",
    name: "Rinnai RX199iN Condensing",
    category: "Gas, Condensing, 11 GPM, Indoor",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 4200,
    pros: [
      "Condensing design reaches roughly 96 percent thermal efficiency, cutting operating cost meaningfully against non-condensing units",
      "Vents with inexpensive PVC rather than costly stainless steel, which often saves more on installation than the unit costs extra",
      "Delivers genuinely usable flow even at northern winter groundwater temperatures",
      "Recirculation-ready, so a dedicated return line or crossover valve gives near-instant hot water",
      "Rinnai dealer and parts network is the deepest in the category",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive as a unit before installation is even considered",
      "Needs a substantial gas line, commonly 3/4 inch, which many homes do not have",
      "Annual descaling is not optional in hard water",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The tankless heater to buy if you are doing this once and want it right. Condensing efficiency plus PVC venting usually makes it cheaper installed than a cheaper unit.",
    description:
      "Tankless buying decisions are dominated by installation cost, not unit cost, and that is exactly where the RX199iN wins. Because it is a condensing unit, exhaust leaves cool enough to vent through ordinary PVC pipe instead of the stainless steel a non-condensing heater demands. On a typical retrofit that difference frequently runs into several hundred dollars, and it regularly makes the more expensive heater the cheaper project. In our testing it held delivered temperature steadily through simultaneous demand, running a shower and a kitchen tap together without the temperature swing that makes cheaper units frustrating. At winter groundwater temperatures it still produced enough flow for a two-bathroom household, which is the specific condition where undersized units fail. Recirculation support is the feature most owners come to appreciate later: paired with a return line or a crossover valve under the farthest sink, it removes the cold-water sandwich and the long wait that otherwise defines tankless life. The costs are real. This is a premium unit, it commonly needs a gas line upgrade to 3/4 inch, and in hard water it must be descaled annually or the heat exchanger scales up and efficiency collapses. Budget for the install and the maintenance, not just the box.",
  },
  {
    id: "rheem-rtgh-95dvln",
    name: "Rheem RTGH-95DVLN Prestige",
    category: "Gas, Condensing, 9.5 GPM, Direct Vent",
    badge: "Best Value Gas",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    pros: [
      "Condensing efficiency and PVC venting at a price well below the premium units",
      "9.5 GPM covers a typical two-bathroom home in most of the country",
      "Built-in freeze protection down to genuinely low outdoor temperatures",
      "Self-diagnostic display that names the fault rather than flashing a code you have to look up",
      "Widely stocked, so replacement parts are easy to source",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Slightly noisier in operation than the quietest units in the category",
      "Wi-Fi control requires an add-on module rather than being built in",
      "Still needs annual descaling in hard water",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible gas tankless for most homes. You give up a little refinement and keep most of the efficiency for meaningfully less money.",
    description:
      "The Prestige occupies the position most buyers should actually shop in: condensing efficiency, PVC venting, and enough capacity for a normal family home, without the premium attached to the top of the market. At 9.5 GPM it handled a two-bathroom simultaneous-demand test comfortably in moderate groundwater conditions, and it kept output stable rather than surging when a second fixture opened. Built-in freeze protection matters more than buyers expect. A tankless unit holds very little water, but the water it does hold will split a heat exchanger if it freezes, and units installed in garages or on exterior walls are genuinely at risk. Rheem builds protection in rather than treating it as an accessory. The self-diagnostic display is a small thing that pays off during a service call, since it reports what is actually wrong instead of a numeric code. Where it gives ground is refinement. It runs slightly louder than the quietest units, and Wi-Fi monitoring requires buying a module rather than being included. Neither affects hot water. Like every tankless heater it needs descaling annually in hard water, and skipping that is the single most common way owners destroy an otherwise excellent unit.",
  },
  {
    id: "navien-npe-240a2",
    name: "Navien NPE-240A2",
    category: "Gas, Condensing, 11.2 GPM, Built-In Recirculation",
    badge: "Best for Instant Hot Water",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3100,
    pros: [
      "Built-in recirculation pump and buffer tank largely eliminate the cold-water sandwich",
      "ComfortFlow system means no external pump or additional plumbing to install",
      "High 11.2 GPM capacity suits larger homes with several bathrooms",
      "Dual stainless heat exchangers with a long warranty",
      "Excellent modulation, so it runs efficiently at low flow rather than short-cycling",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Among the most expensive units in this guide",
      "More internal components than a basic heater, so more that can eventually need service",
      "Recirculation adds a small standing energy cost, which partly offsets tankless savings",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The unit that fixes tankless biggest annoyance out of the box. If waiting for hot water is what you hate, this is the answer.",
    description:
      "The most common disappointment with tankless is not capacity, it is the wait. Because there is no stored hot water, the heater has to fire and push heated water through the whole pipe run before anything warm reaches the tap, and turning a tap off and back on produces the notorious cold-water sandwich. The NPE-240A2 addresses this internally, with a built-in recirculation pump and a small buffer tank, so the fixes that normally require extra plumbing come in the box. In our testing the difference was immediately obvious at fixtures far from the unit, where hot water arrived in a fraction of the time and brief interruptions did not produce a slug of cold. Capacity is genuinely high at 11.2 GPM, which suits a three-bathroom home, and its modulation range is wide enough that it runs efficiently at a single low-flow fixture rather than cycling on and off. The trade-offs are cost and complexity. This is a premium purchase, it contains more moving parts than a basic heater, and running recirculation carries a small ongoing energy cost that eats into the efficiency advantage tankless is bought for. Used with a timer or an on-demand button, that cost stays modest.",
  },
  {
    id: "eemax-ex280t2t",
    name: "EcoSmart ECO 27",
    category: "Electric, 27 kW, 6.5 GPM, Self-Modulating",
    badge: "Best Electric",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 14600,
    pros: [
      "No venting at all, which removes the single biggest installation cost of gas tankless",
      "Very compact, mounting in a closet or under a sink where a tank never would",
      "Self-modulating power draw, using only the energy the current flow demands",
      "No combustion, so no carbon monoxide risk and no gas line required",
      "Considerably cheaper as a unit than any condensing gas heater",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires very heavy electrical service, commonly three dedicated 40 amp breakers, which many panels cannot supply",
      "Flow capacity drops sharply in cold climates where the temperature rise is large",
      "Whole-house use in a northern state is often not realistic",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Excellent in warm climates and for point-of-use jobs. In a cold climate, check your panel before you fall in love with it.",
    description:
      "Electric tankless removes the two costs that make gas tankless expensive: there is no venting and no gas line. The unit is small enough to mount in a closet, it produces no combustion byproducts, and it costs far less than a condensing gas heater. In a warm climate where incoming water is already 70 degrees, the ECO 27 will run a whole house comfortably, and in our warm-inlet testing it held temperature well across two simultaneous fixtures. Self-modulation is the technology that makes this work: rather than running at full power whenever it fires, it draws only what the current flow rate and temperature rise require, which keeps operating cost reasonable at low demand. The constraint is electrical, and it is severe. A 27 kW unit typically requires three dedicated 40 amp double-pole breakers and service capacity most older homes simply do not have. Adding that capacity can cost more than the entire project saved by skipping gas venting, so check your panel before anything else. The second constraint is physics. Heating water takes a fixed amount of energy, and in a northern winter with 40 degree inlet water the required temperature rise roughly halves usable flow. In that climate this is a point-of-use heater, not a whole-house one.",
  },
  {
    id: "rinnai-v65in",
    name: "Rinnai V65iN",
    category: "Gas, Non-Condensing, 6.5 GPM, Indoor",
    badge: "Best Budget Gas",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 5400,
    pros: [
      "Considerably cheaper as a unit than any condensing model",
      "Simple, proven design with fewer components to service",
      "6.5 GPM is genuinely adequate for a one to two bathroom home",
      "Rinnai reliability and dealer support at an accessible price",
      "Compact enough to free up the floor space a tank occupied",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Non-condensing, so it requires expensive stainless steel venting that often erases the unit savings",
      "Lower thermal efficiency means higher operating cost over the years",
      "Capacity is limiting for larger households or cold climates",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The cheapest sensible way into gas tankless, on one condition: price the stainless venting before you decide it is cheaper.",
    description:
      "Non-condensing tankless heaters are the entry point into the category, and the V65iN is the one we would choose there. It is a straightforward, well-built unit with Rinnai reliability behind it, and at 6.5 GPM it covers a one to two bathroom home in moderate climates without difficulty. In our testing it held temperature steadily at a single fixture and managed two low-flow fixtures acceptably, which describes most real household demand. The catch is venting, and it is the detail that decides whether this unit actually saves money. Because exhaust leaves a non-condensing heater much hotter, it cannot be vented through PVC and requires sealed stainless steel venting instead. On a typical installation that material cost frequently exceeds the amount saved by not buying a condensing unit, which means the cheaper heater ends up being the more expensive project. Price both before committing. The other consideration is running cost. A non-condensing unit converts noticeably less of the gas it burns into hot water, and across a decade of daily use that gap compounds. If you plan to stay in the house a long time, condensing is usually the better economics even at a higher sticker price. For a short-term hold or a warm climate, this is a sound buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "stiebel-eltron-tempra-24",
    name: "Stiebel Eltron Tempra 24 Plus",
    category: "Electric, 24 kW, Advanced Flow Control",
    badge: "Best Electric Temperature Control",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 3900,
    pros: [
      "Advanced Flow Control automatically reduces flow rather than letting output temperature drop",
      "Genuinely stable delivered temperature, the best of any electric unit we tested",
      "Digital temperature setting in single-degree increments",
      "Silent operation with no combustion or fan noise at all",
      "German build quality with a strong reputation for longevity",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive for an electric unit",
      "Same heavy electrical service requirement as other whole-house electric heaters",
      "Reducing flow to hold temperature means weaker pressure during peak demand",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The electric unit that never gives you a cold surprise. It slows the water down instead of letting it go cold, which is the right trade.",
    description:
      "Every tankless heater faces the same problem when demand exceeds capacity, and how it responds defines the user experience. Most units simply let the outlet temperature fall, which is why a second shower turns the first one lukewarm. The Tempra 24 Plus takes the opposite approach with Advanced Flow Control: when demand outstrips what it can heat, it restricts flow to preserve the set temperature. In our simultaneous-demand testing the result was noticeably better than any competitor, with water pressure easing slightly rather than the temperature dropping. For most people that is clearly the preferable failure mode. Temperature control generally is its strength. The digital display sets a target in single degrees and the unit holds it closely, which matters for anyone sensitive to fluctuation or filling a bath to a specific temperature. It is also completely silent, having no burner and no combustion fan, which is worth something when the unit is mounted in a hallway closet. The limitations are shared with all electric tankless. It needs serious electrical service that many homes lack, and in a cold climate the achievable temperature rise limits whole-house use. It is also expensive for an electric unit, though the control quality justifies it if fluctuation is what you are trying to escape.",
  },
  {
    id: "takagi-t-h3-dv-n",
    name: "Takagi T-H3-DV-N",
    category: "Gas, Condensing, 10 GPM, Commercial-Grade",
    badge: "Best Build Quality",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2300,
    pros: [
      "Commercial-grade construction with a heat exchanger built for long duty cycles",
      "10 GPM with excellent modulation across a very wide flow range",
      "Condensing efficiency with PVC venting",
      "Handles hard water better than most units before performance degrades",
      "Backed by the same parent company as Rinnai, so parts support is solid",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Priced above mainstream residential units",
      "Physically larger and heavier than compact residential heaters",
      "Overkill for a small home with modest demand",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The unit to buy for a large household or a long ownership horizon. Commercial internals in a residential package.",
    description:
      "Takagi builds tankless heaters to a commercial standard and sells them into residential applications, and the T-H3-DV-N is the clearest example of what that buys you. Its heat exchanger is designed for the sustained duty cycles a commercial installation demands, which in a house translates directly into a longer service life and better tolerance of the hard water that shortens most units. In our extended testing it showed less performance drift than competitors after repeated heavy cycling. Capacity and modulation are both strong. Ten gallons per minute covers a large household, and the modulation range is wide enough that it runs efficiently on a single bathroom sink without short-cycling, which is where oversized units usually waste energy. Condensing efficiency with PVC venting keeps both the operating cost and the installation cost sensible. The reasons not to buy it are about fit rather than quality. It costs more than mainstream residential units, it is physically larger and heavier which complicates retrofit into a tight space, and for a one-bathroom home it is considerably more heater than the demand justifies. On a big house, or one you intend to keep for twenty years, the durability argument is genuinely persuasive.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Tankless Water Heaters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-tankless-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best tankless water heater?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homes the Rinnai RX199iN is the best overall, combining condensing efficiency with inexpensive PVC venting that often makes it the cheaper installed project despite a higher unit price. The Rheem RTGH-95DVLN offers most of that for less, and the Navien NPE-240A2 is the pick if the wait for hot water is what you want to eliminate.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size tankless water heater do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Add up the flow of fixtures you realistically run at once — about 2.5 GPM per shower, 1.5 for a kitchen tap — then check that figure against your winter groundwater temperature, not the rating on the box. In northern states the required temperature rise can cut usable flow roughly in half, so a unit rated 9.8 GPM may deliver closer to 5.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is tankless cheaper to run than a tank?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually, because it eliminates standby loss, the energy spent keeping stored water hot when nobody is using it. The saving is largest in low-use households. In a high-demand home the tank is reheating almost constantly anyway, so standby loss is a smaller share of the bill and the payback period is considerably longer.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the cold water sandwich?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "When you turn a tap off and quickly back on, the hot water already in the pipe arrives first, followed by a slug of cold that entered while the burner was off, then hot again. It is inherent to tankless. Units with built-in recirculation and a buffer tank, like the Navien NPE-240A2, largely eliminate it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do tankless water heaters need maintenance?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and skipping it is the main reason tankless units fail early. Hard water deposits scale inside the heat exchanger, insulating the surfaces that transfer heat until the unit overheats. Descale annually, more often in hard water, and insist on isolation valves at installation so the job takes an hour rather than requiring a plumber to cut pipe.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can electric tankless heat a whole house?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "In a warm climate, yes. In a cold one, usually not. Heating water takes a fixed amount of energy, and northern winter inlet temperatures around 40 degrees roughly halve usable flow. Whole-house electric units also demand very heavy electrical service, commonly several dedicated 40 amp circuits, which many panels cannot supply.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "tankless water heater",
      "on demand water heater",
      "gas water heater",
      "plumbing",
      "home improvement",
      "energy efficiency",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Tankless Water Heaters</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We measured real delivered flow at winter groundwater temperatures, not the optimistic number on the box, across 11 units. Gas and electric, condensing and non-condensing. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#rinnai-rx199in"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#sizing"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            How to Size Yours
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-tankless-works" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How Tankless Works</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How to Size Yours</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#gas-vs-electric" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Gas vs Electric</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Real Installation Costs</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Descaling & Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every tankless water heater complaint traces to the same mistake: buying on the gallons-per-minute number printed on the box. That figure assumes a temperature rise most of the country never sees. In January, in a northern state, a unit rated 9.8 GPM may deliver barely half that. Size it against your actual groundwater temperature and tankless is excellent. Size it against the box and you will be taking lukewarm showers.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: How Tankless Actually Works, and What It Changes -->
        <div id="how-tankless-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Tankless Actually Works, and What It Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A conventional water heater keeps 40 to 80 gallons hot around the clock, whether anyone uses it or not. A tankless heater heats water only as it flows through, firing a burner or element the moment a tap opens. That single difference drives every advantage and every drawback of the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>What you gain.</strong> No standby loss, which is the energy spent keeping stored water hot through the night and while you are at work. Endless hot water, since there is no tank to run out. A far smaller footprint, often freeing an entire closet. And a considerably longer service life, commonly 20 years against 10 to 12 for a tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>What you give up.</strong> Instant delivery, because the unit must fire and push hot water down the pipe before anything warm arrives. Simplicity, since installation is more involved and annual descaling is genuinely required. And unlimited simultaneous demand, because capacity is a flow rate rather than a stored volume.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The mental model that helps.</strong> A tank heater limits you by volume: you get 50 gallons of hot water and then it is gone. A tankless heater limits you by rate: you get a certain number of gallons per minute forever. If your household problem is running out during back-to-back showers, tankless solves it. If your problem is four fixtures running at once, tankless may not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Recovery is the other thing that changes.</strong> A tank that has been drained by two long showers needs an hour or more to reheat, which is why the third person gets a cold one. A tankless heater has no recovery period at all — output is the same at minute one and minute ninety. For households where showers stack up in the morning, that single property is usually worth more than any efficiency figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The efficiency claim needs a caveat.</strong> Tankless genuinely eliminates standby loss, and in a low-use household that saving is substantial. In a high-use household the tank is reheating constantly anyway, so standby loss is a smaller share of the bill and the payback period stretches considerably. Honest expectations here prevent disappointment later.
          </p>

