          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Sizing: The Number on the Box Is Not Your Number
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where most tankless purchases go wrong, and it is entirely avoidable with five minutes of arithmetic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity Depends on Temperature Rise</h3>

          <p>
            A tankless heater has a fixed heating power. How many gallons per minute it can deliver depends entirely on how much it has to raise the temperature. The headline GPM figure is almost always quoted at a small temperature rise that suits a warm climate. Raise the required rise and the achievable flow falls proportionally.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Find Your Groundwater Temperature</h3>

          <p>
            Incoming water runs roughly 65 to 75 degrees across the southern states and 35 to 50 degrees across the northern tier in winter. If you want 120 degree output, a Florida household needs a rise of about 50 degrees while a Minnesota household needs about 80. That is the difference between a unit delivering its rated flow and delivering little more than half of it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add Up Your Actual Fixtures</h3>

          <p>
            A modern shower head flows about 2.5 gallons per minute, a kitchen tap about 1.5, a bathroom tap about 1, and a dishwasher or washing machine 1 to 2. Decide which fixtures realistically run at once. Two showers together is 5 GPM. A shower plus a kitchen tap is 4. Size to that number at your winter temperature rise, not to the box.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Oversize Blindly Either</h3>

          <p>
            A hugely oversized unit costs more, may need a larger gas line, and can struggle to modulate down far enough to run a single low-flow fixture without cycling. Match the unit to realistic peak demand rather than to the worst case you can imagine.
          </p>

