        <!-- SECTION 4: Gas vs Electric Tankless -->
        <div id="gas-vs-electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas vs Electric Tankless
          </h2>

          <p>
            The fuel decision is mostly an infrastructure decision, and it is usually settled by what your house already has rather than by which is better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Tankless</h3>

          <p>
            Higher capacity, better suited to whole-house use in any climate, and cheaper to run per gallon in most of the country. The costs are installation: a substantial gas line, often an upgrade to 3/4 inch, plus venting. Gas is the default for whole-house tankless and the only realistic option in a cold climate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Tankless</h3>

          <p>
            No venting, no gas line, no combustion, and a much smaller and cheaper unit. The constraint is electrical service, and it is severe: whole-house electric units commonly need multiple dedicated 40 amp circuits and total service capacity that older homes do not have. Excellent in warm climates and outstanding for point-of-use applications.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Condensing vs Non-Condensing, the Sub-Decision That Costs Real Money</h3>

          <p>
            Within gas, condensing units extract additional heat from the exhaust, reaching far higher efficiency and cooling the exhaust enough to vent through ordinary PVC. Non-condensing units are cheaper but require sealed stainless steel venting that frequently costs more than the price difference. Always price the complete installation, not the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Propane is a third case worth naming.</strong> Most gas tankless units are sold in both natural gas and propane versions, and they are not interchangeable without a conversion kit. Propane delivers more energy per unit volume, so a propane unit often achieves a slightly better temperature rise, but fuel cost is typically higher and you are dependent on tank deliveries. If you are off mains gas, confirm the model number matches your fuel before ordering, because returning an installed unit is not straightforward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Point of Use Is an Underrated Option</h3>

          <p>
            A small electric tankless mounted under a distant sink, fed by the existing hot line, removes the long wait at that fixture without re-plumbing the house. For a guest bath or a workshop sink far from the heater, this is often a better answer than a whole-house replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Installation Costs Nobody Quotes You Up Front -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installation Costs Nobody Quotes You Up Front
          </h2>

          <p>
            The unit is frequently less than half the project. These are the costs that surprise people after they have already bought the heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas line sizing.</strong> A tankless heater fires at a far higher rate than a tank, and many homes have a 1/2 inch line that cannot supply it. Upgrading to 3/4 inch back to the meter is common and can be expensive if the run is long or awkward.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Venting.</strong> Condensing units vent through PVC. Non-condensing units need sealed stainless steel, which costs several times as much per foot. The route also matters: a straightforward sidewall vent is cheap, while running vertically through a finished house is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Electrical.</strong> Gas units still need power for the controls and fan, so a nearby circuit is required. Electric units need service that many panels cannot provide, and a panel upgrade is a serious expense that should be priced before purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Condensate drainage.</strong> Condensing units produce acidic condensate that has to be drained and, in many jurisdictions, neutralised before entering the waste system. That means a drain within reach and sometimes a neutraliser cartridge that needs periodic replacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mounting location.</strong> Tankless units are wall-hung and need clearances around them for service access and combustion air. A spot that looks convenient can fail on clearance, on vent routing, or on being too far from the fixtures that matter most. Mounting closer to the bathrooms shortens the wait for hot water more effectively than any feature on the unit, so it is worth walking the house before deciding where it goes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water treatment.</strong> In hard water, a scale filter or softener protects the heat exchanger and dramatically reduces descaling frequency. It is an added cost that pays for itself by preventing the failure that ends most tankless units early.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Price the Project, Not the Product</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Get a full installed quote before choosing between a condensing and non-condensing unit. It is genuinely common for the more expensive condensing heater to produce the cheaper total project, because PVC venting costs so much less than stainless. The unit price is the least useful number in this category.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Descaling and Maintenance -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Descaling and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters last roughly twice as long as tanks, but only if they are maintained. Neglect kills them well before their time, and the mechanism is always the same.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Scale is the enemy.</strong> Hard water deposits calcium inside the heat exchanger, where it insulates the very surfaces that need to transfer heat. As scale builds, the unit works harder for less output, efficiency falls, and eventually the exchanger overheats and fails. This is the single most common cause of premature tankless death.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Descale annually, or more often in hard water.</strong> The process circulates a food-safe descaling solution through the unit for about an hour using a small pump and two hoses. It is genuinely a DIY job if isolation valves were installed, and it is the highest-value hour you will spend on the appliance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Insist on isolation valves at installation.</strong> These are the two service valves that let you connect a descaling pump without cutting pipe. They cost very little as part of the original install and are a real nuisance to add later. If a quote does not include them, ask why.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean the inlet screen.</strong> A small filter at the cold inlet catches sediment, and a clogged one reduces flow in a way that looks exactly like a failing heater. Pull it and rinse it a couple of times a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the venting and condensate line.</strong> Look for obstructions, and in cold climates confirm the condensate line cannot freeze. A blocked condensate line will shut a condensing unit down, usually on the coldest night of the year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes the Rinnai RX199iN is the best overall, combining condensing efficiency with inexpensive PVC venting that often makes it the cheaper installed project despite a higher unit price. The Rheem RTGH-95DVLN offers most of that for less, and the Navien NPE-240A2 is the pick if the wait for hot water is what you want to eliminate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size tankless water heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add up the flow of fixtures you realistically run at once — about 2.5 GPM per shower, 1.5 for a kitchen tap — then check that figure against your winter groundwater temperature, not the rating on the box. In northern states the required temperature rise can cut usable flow roughly in half, so a unit rated 9.8 GPM may deliver closer to 5.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is tankless cheaper to run than a tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, because it eliminates standby loss, the energy spent keeping stored water hot when nobody is using it. The saving is largest in low-use households. In a high-demand home the tank is reheating almost constantly anyway, so standby loss is a smaller share of the bill and the payback period is considerably longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cold water sandwich?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                When you turn a tap off and quickly back on, the hot water already in the pipe arrives first, followed by a slug of cold that entered while the burner was off, then hot again. It is inherent to tankless. Units with built-in recirculation and a buffer tank, like the Navien NPE-240A2, largely eliminate it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do tankless water heaters need maintenance?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and skipping it is the main reason tankless units fail early. Hard water deposits scale inside the heat exchanger, insulating the surfaces that transfer heat until the unit overheats. Descale annually, more often in hard water, and insist on isolation valves at installation so the job takes an hour rather than requiring a plumber to cut pipe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can electric tankless heat a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In a warm climate, yes. In a cold one, usually not. Heating water takes a fixed amount of energy, and northern winter inlet temperatures around 40 degrees roughly halve usable flow. Whole-house electric units also demand very heavy electrical service, commonly several dedicated 40 amp circuits, which many panels cannot supply.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Tankless Water Heater Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tankless disappointment is remarkably consistent, and it almost always happens before the unit is ever switched on.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing From the Number on the Box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The advertised GPM assumes a small temperature rise that most of the country never experiences. In a northern winter the same unit may deliver half its rated flow. Work out your actual groundwater temperature and your realistic simultaneous demand, then size to that. This one calculation prevents the majority of tankless regret.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Unit Prices Instead of Installed Prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A non-condensing heater is cheaper on the shelf and needs stainless steel venting that frequently costs more than the saving. The condensing unit that looked expensive often produces the cheaper total project. Always compare complete installed quotes, including gas line sizing and condensate drainage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Isolation Valves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                These two small service valves are what make annual descaling a one-hour DIY job instead of a plumbing call requiring cut pipe. They cost very little when fitted during the original installation and are a genuine nuisance to retrofit. If they are missing from a quote, add them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Descaling in Hard Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scale insulates the heat exchanger, efficiency falls, the unit works harder, and eventually it overheats and fails. This is the single most common cause of a tankless heater dying years before it should. Descale annually, and consider a scale filter or softener if your water is genuinely hard.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

